WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.760
Film Courage: Larry do you remember the first
time that you pitched one of your screenplay

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:07.540
ideas to a producer and it worked?

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:14.060
Larry Wilson: Yes…I do.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:19.060
And it was to a producer named Barry Cross
and it was an original idea of mine.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:24.429
I’ve gotten to the point where pitching
comes pretty easily to me because I’ve been

00:00:24.429 --> 00:00:28.679
on both sides of the desk and I know the drill
and I know how to do it.

00:00:28.679 --> 00:00:37.620
But at first it was incredibly difficult and
it was always like these sweaty armpits.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:43.289
And as you know…what I always worry about
with these interviews is that everyone hears

00:00:43.289 --> 00:00:46.059
me stammering, you know?

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:58.720
[Laughs] But I remember telling this story
and he was engaged and he was listening and

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:06.500
I’ve had pitches where (I know everyone
has had these) two minutes in, it’s just

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:12.110
a slow death march basically and you just
want to get it over with and say goodbye and

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:17.680
get out of the room but he was actually engaged
and listening and I knew it was working because

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.829
then he started asking me questions.

00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:28.260
And they weren’t the polite “here’s
a couple questions, now go away!”

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:35.270
He actually was curious and it was a great
feeling and it allowed me to overcome a kind

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:45.820
of a…I used to say that I started teaching
so I’d learn to talk in front of people

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:53.170
and now you can’t shut me up because there
was a lot of shyness I had to overcome and

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:59.969
so sitting in front of someone and talking
at that point in my life wasn’t easy.

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:00.969
But I could feel the eye contact.

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:06.140
I could feel him engaged in the story and
again when he started asking me questions

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.519
that told me he had really been listening,
that was a great feeling.

00:02:10.519 --> 00:02:18.870
And that made pitching easier until the next
time I went to pitch and it all went horribly.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:30.540
I’ve had them go both ways but I’ve developed
a very thick skin about it now.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:37.010
A couple years ago I pitched an idea to Russell
Brand’s development company.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:40.680
I think he’s…I don’t know if he has
that company still?

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.650
But I was told by his executive that it was
too far out for Russell Brand and I thought

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.540
“It’s too far out for Russell Brand?

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.540
I’m ruined!”

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:50.970
How does that happen?

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:56.770
And so that one, I went away going “Wow!”

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.920
Acid flashback I guess or something?

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.349
Film Courage: Timothy Leary would have gotten
it.

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:05.459
Larry Wilson.

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:06.459
Right!

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:12.720
So pitch meetings can go both ways.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:21.200
What I’ve developed over the years is a
real sense of humor about this stuff and there’s

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:31.650
a great philosopher who has been a touchstone
for me forever named Alan Watts and he once

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:41.310
said (it’s almost like a riff on Jimmy Page’s
‘Don’t be respectable, be responsible’)

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:44.760
but he said “Don’t be serious, be sincere.”

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:51.299
And I think that is so true and I never go
into these meetings trying to con someone

00:03:51.299 --> 00:03:57.220
or tell them something I don’t believe in.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:01.560
And I treat them very sincerely but I’ve
learned not to take them seriously because

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:08.829
I’ve seen people and I could actually tell
some very dark stories about people who have

00:04:08.829 --> 00:04:19.130
taken this stuff way too seriously and it
has literally destroyed them and I refuse

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.700
to have a body count that like anymore in
my life.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.880
So you go in and you do your best and you
tell them a story and you think you believe

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:30.320
in it and if it’s too far out for Russell
Brand, what can I say?

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:31.950
I’m sorry.

